                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/infinity-limits+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#classification"} Classification
              %li
                %a{:href => "#pseudo_limits_and_homotopy_limits"} Pseudo limits and homotopy limits
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#existence_and_construction"} Existence and construction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In general, a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit"} 2-limit
            is the sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            appropriate in a (weak)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            we also have another notion at our disposal:
          %p
            Since strict 2-categories are equivalently categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            (i.e. over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/very+large+category"} very large
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            between them), we can apply the usual notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limits"} weighted limits
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            verbatim to speak of certain 2-limits (
            %a{:href => "#Street76"}> Street 1976
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kelly89"} Kelly 1989
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limits"} weighted limits
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %em strict 2-limits
            %em 2-limits
            while the properly 2-category-theoretic limits were called
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em bilimits
          %p
            Because
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            doesn’t know anything about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theoretic
            nature of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %em strict 2-limits
            can have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cones"} cones
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commute
            strictly and have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+properties"} universal properties
            expressed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            of categories (instead of
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theoretic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %p
            However, strict 2-limits often turn out to be technically useful even if one is ultimately interested only in weak 2-limits, since in many situations strict 2-limits may serve as stepping stones for the construction of weak
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limits"} 2-limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            as a tool to get at weak ones, and in fact a precise comparison can be made (see below).
          %h2#classification Classification
          %p
            By a
            %strong limit
            we will mean the fully 2-categorical notion described at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit"} 2-limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commute
            up to invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            is expressed by an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          .query
            %p It just occurred to me that ‘strict initial object’ conflicts with this. But unlike ‘weak limit’, that doesn’t generalise very far.
            %p
              Heh, you’re right. I suppose we could try calling strict initial objects
              = succeed "," do
                %em stable initial objects
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong strict 2-limit
                (or just
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em strict limit
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong pseudo limit
                (or
                %em strict pseudo limit
                if it is necessary to emphasize the strictness) is a limit whose cones commute up to coherent 2-cell isomorphism, but whose universal property can still be expressed by an
                %em isomorphism
                of categories. For any weight
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'
                (a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+replacement"} cofibrant replacement
                of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                = succeed "-weighted" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                = succeed "-weighted" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'
                includes explicitly all the extra isomorphisms in a pseudo
                = succeed "-cone." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %em a fortiori
                an equivalence of categories, any pseudo limit is also a limit.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong strict lax limit
                is a limit whose cones commute only up to a coherent transformation in one direction, but again whose universal property is expressed by an isomorphism. Likewise we have
                %strong strict oplax limits
                where the transformation goes in the other direction. Strict lax and oplax limits can also be rephrased as strict (non-lax) limits for a different weight. As in the pseudo case, any strict (op)lax limit is also an (op)lax limit.
          %p
            More generally, any strict limit that respects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            (one which doesn’t demand equality of objects) will also be a limit. The strict limits corresponding to non-strict limits are precisely the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiflexible+limits"} semiflexible limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flexible+limits"} flexible limits
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PIE-limits"} PIE-limits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MonCat"} MonCat
          %h2#pseudo_limits_and_homotopy_limits Pseudo limits and homotopy limits
          %p
            If there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure on the 1-category underlying the given strict 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+2-category"} model 2-category
            with the “trivial” or “natural” model structure constructed in (Lack 2006), then these two notions coincide (Gambino 2007). For example, this is the case in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            are also examples of pseudo limits. In general, homotopy limits in a model 2-category give (non-strict) limits in its “homotopy 2-category.”
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Any ordinary 1-limit can be made into a strict 2-limit simply by boosting up its ordinary universal property (a bijection of sets) to an isomorphism of hom-categories. Thus we have strict
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+objects"} terminal objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            (and thus are also 2-limits), while others such as pullbacks and equalizers tend to violate the 2-category theoretic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For example, a strict terminal object is an object 1 such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X,1)
                is
                %em isomorphic
                to the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                Likewise, a strict product of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is an object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
                with projections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:A\times B\to A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:A\times B\to B
                such that (1) given any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to A
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:X\to B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:X\to A\times B
                such thath
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mi h
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p h = f
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mi h
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q h = g
                (equal, not isomorphic) (i.e.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                in the underlying
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h,k:X\to A\times B
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi p
                      %mi h
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: p h \to p k
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo :
                        %mi q
                        %mi h
                        %mo →
                        %mi q
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta:q h \to q k
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo :
                      %mi h
                      %mo →
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma:h\to k
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mi γ
                      %mo =
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\gamma = \alpha
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi q
                        %mi γ
                        %mo =
                        %mi β
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\gamma =\beta
          %p
            As mentioned above, adding
            %strong pseudo
            in front of an ordinary limit has a precise meaning: it means that all the triangles in the limit cone now commute up to specified isomorphism, and the universal property is still expressed by an isomorphism of categories. In particular, there is still a specified projection to
            %em each
            object in the diagram. For example:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  pseudo
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                of a cospan
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi C
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi g
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} C \overset{g}{\leftarrow} B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                equipped with projections
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:P\to A
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi q
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:P\to B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r:P\to C
                and 2-cell isomorphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi p
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f p \cong r
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mi q
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g q \cong r
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  pseudo
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
                of a pair of arrows
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:A\rightrightarrows B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                equipped with morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:E\to A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k:E\to B
                and 2-cell isomorphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi h
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f h \cong k
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mi h
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g h \cong k
          %p These are to be distinguished from:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  iso-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} comma object
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi C
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi g
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} C \overset{g}{\leftarrow} B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                equipped with projections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:P\to A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo :
                      %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:P\to B
                and a 2-cell isomorphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mi p
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi g
                        %mi q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f p \cong g q
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  iso-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inserter"} inserter
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:A\rightrightarrows B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                equipped with a morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:E\to A
                and a 2-cell isomorphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mi e
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi g
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f e \cong g e
          %p
            The pseudo pullback, pseudo equalizer, iso-comma object, and iso-inserter are all strict Cat-weighted limits; their universal property is expressed by an isomorphism of categories. Usually the pseudo pullback and iso-comma object are not isomorphic, and likewise the pseudo equalizer and iso-inserter are not isomorphic. However, both the pseudo pullback and iso-comma object respect the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            and represent a pullback; therefore they are
            %em equivalent
            when they both exist. Likewise, the pseudo equalizer and iso-inserter both represent an equalizer, and are equivalent when they both exist.
          %p
            If one is mostly interested in (non-strict) limits, then there is little harm in using “pseudo pullback” to mean “iso-comma object” or “pullback,” as is common in the literature. However, with
            %em lax
            limits the situation is more serious. Speaking precisely, in the
            %em lax
            version of a limit, the triangles in the limiting cone are made to commute up to a specified transformation in one direction, but there are still specified projections to
            %em each
            object in the diagram. For example:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong strict lax limit of an arrow
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                equipped with projections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi L
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:L\to A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo :
                      %mi L
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:L\to B
                and a 2-cell
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mi p
                        %mo →
                        %mi q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f p \to q
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong strict lax pullback
                of a cospan
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi C
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi g
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} C \overset{g}{\leftarrow} B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                equipped with projections
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:P\to A
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi q
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:P\to B
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r:P\to C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f p \to r
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mi q
                        %mo →
                        %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g q \to r
          %p In particular, the strict lax pullback in the following list of examples is quite different from the following more common limit.
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %strong comma object
              of a cospan
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi g
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \overset{f}{\to} C \overset{g}{\leftarrow} B
              is a generalization of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
              in
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f/g)
              equipped with projections
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:(f/g)\to A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:(f/g)\to B
              and a 2-cell
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi p
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f p \to g q
          %p Even in their non-strict forms, the lax pullback and comma object are distinct. Usually the comma object is the more important one, but calling it a “lax pullback” should be avoided.
          %p
            Here are some more important examples of 2-limits, all of which come in strict and weak forms and respect the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power"} power
                of an object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                by a category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^C
                equipped with a functor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to K(A^C,A)
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inserter"} inserter
                of a pair of parallel arrows
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:A \;\rightrightarrows\; B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                equipped with a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi I
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:I\to A
                and a 2-cell
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f i \to g i
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverter"} inverter
                of a 2-cell
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi f
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:f\to g:A\to B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                with a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v:V\to A
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha v
                is invertible.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    identifier
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/identifier"} ?
                of a 2-cell
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi f
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:f\to g:A\to B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                with a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v:V\to A
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha v
                is the identity.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equifier"} equifier
                of a pair of parallel 2-cells
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ,
                      %mi β
                      %mo :
                      %mi f
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha,\beta: f\to g: A\to B
                is a universal object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                equipped with a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e:E\to A
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mi e
                        %mo =
                        %mi β
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha e = \beta e
          %h2#existence_and_construction Existence and construction
          %p
            Strict 2-limits can be constructed from one another. We record some fact about this from (
            %a{:href => "#Kelly89"}> Kelly ‘89
            ).
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Every strict 2-limit can be constructed out of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+limits"} conical limits
              (which, in turn, can be constructed by either
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+objects"} terminal objects
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powers"} powers
              by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+arrow"} walking arrow
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              This is a special case of the fact that
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒱
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒱
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powers"} powers
              alone. Since
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-categories"} 2-categories
              are
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cat}
              = succeed "-limits," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cat}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+arrow"} walking arrow
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+generator"} strong generator
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cat}
          %p
            Fix a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              admits binary products and (iso)inserters, it also admits (iso)commas.
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              admits (iso)commas, it also admits (pseudo)co/lax limits of arrows, and powers by the walking arrow (the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+isomorphism"} walking isomorphism
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{I}
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
            %ol
              %li
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
                admits inserters and identifiers, it also admits equalizers.
              %li
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
                admits commas and identifiers, it also admits pullbacks,
              %li
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒦
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
                admits commas and pullbacks, it also admits identifiers.
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Claim (3) is not from Kelly, but it can be easily verified that the identifier of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:f \Rightarrow g:A \to B
              is given by taking the pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo ′
                        %mi α
                        %mo ′
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                    %mo ←
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \xrightarrow{'\alpha'} f/g \leftarrow f \times_B g
              where both maps are universal comparison maps induced by the comma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f/g
              (one by the lax square
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p
            This is Proposition 4.2 in
            = succeed "," do
              %em ibid.
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Bird"} Bird
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              (a) If equifiers exist in a 2-category, so do endo
              %em> identifiers
              ;
              %br>/
              (b) if inserters and endo
              %em identifiers
              exist, so do inverters;
              %br>/
              (c) if inserters and inverters exist, so do isoinserters.
            %p
              Hence a 2-category with inserters and equifiers admits endo-
              %em> identifiers
              , inverters, and isoinserters; if it also admits binary products, it admits commas and isocommas, the lax limit, the colax limit, and the pseudolimit of an arrow, and the powers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⋔
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2} \pitchfork B
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi I
                      %mo ⋔
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{I} \pitchfork B
          %p
            This is Proposition 4.3 in
            = succeed ":" do
              %em ibid.
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              admits products, inserters, and equifiers, it admits powers.
          %p
            This is Proposition 5.2 in
            = succeed ":" do
              %em ibid.
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              admits products, inserters, and equifiers, it admits all pseudo
              %em and
              lax limits.
          %p
            The following is Lemma A.2 in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Tendas2024"} Tendas 2024
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              admits (finite)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+coequalisers"} reflexive coequalisers
          %h2#references References
          %p Original articles on Cat-enriched weighted limits:
          %ul
            %li#Street76
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Limits indexed by category-valued 2-functors
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(76)90013-X"} doi:10.1016/0022-4049(76)90013-X
            %li#Kelly89
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Elementary observations on 2-categorical limits
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0004972700002781"} doi:10.1017/S0004972700002781
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/C14F8C3C46D45BCEC748660630EA7535/S0004972700002781a.pdf/div-class-title-elementary-observations-on-2-categorical-limits-div.pdf"} pdf
          %p Review and list of examples:
          %ul
            %li#Lack10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"}> Steve Lack
                , around Sec. 6.8 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A 2-categories companion
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Towards+Higher+Categories"} Towards Higher Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0702535"} arXiv:math.CT/0702535
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-1524-5_4"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4419-1524-5_4
            %li#JohnsonYau20
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niles+Johnson"}> Niles Johnson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Donald+Yau"} Donald Yau
                = succeed "," do
                  %em 2-Dimensional Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/2002.06055"} arXiv:2002.06055
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://oxford.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.1093/oso/9780198871378.001.0001/oso-9780198871378"} doi:10.1093/oso/9780198871378.001.0001
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"}> Steve Lack
                ,
                %em Homotopy theoretic aspects of 2-monads
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.arxiv.org/abs/math.CT/0607646"}> arXiv:math.CT/0607646
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicola+Gambino"}> Nicola Gambino
                ,
                %em Homotopy limits for 2-categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www1.maths.leeds.ac.uk/~pmtng/Publications/homotopy.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Tendas2024
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giacomo+Tendas"}> Giacomo Tendas
                ,
                = succeed "," do
